{"signature": "", "thinking": "This is a pessimistic result: with 50% and 33% rates and ten items, the failure chance is about 16%, so accuracy is the real constraint and F1 comes second. Eight items would bring the risk down to about 10%, which means the test set needs to be around five or six items for under 5% risk. The open question is what N_test is for our models, so I'll try to pin that down from the de-biased noise estimate.\n\nI'm now setting up a per-item estimate of how the pairwise margin shifts, de-biased by removing the group-dependent mean shift, so that the noise is roughly symmetric. From there I want to turn that noise distribution into a flip probability for each practice item, and scale the result by an assumed test-to-practice margin density to estimate N_test for a few values of k.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}
